```markdown
# MyNodeEditor – Professional Data Flow Node Editor (Qt 6.11)

**Target UI Style**: Unreal Engine Blueprints / Blender Node Editor
**Goal**: Clean, modern, dark-themed node editor with automatic **data flow** processing.

---

## Project Goal

Create a reusable, high-quality node editor where nodes process and automatically propagate data downstream when inputs change.
The UI should feel professional like Unreal Engine Blueprints or Blender:
- Dark theme
- Left palette/sidebar
- Central zoomable pannable canvas
- Right properties panel
- Top menu bar
- Optional minimap and status bar

---

## 1. Technology Choice (Recommended)

**Qt Quick (QML) + C++ Backend**

**Why this is the best approach**:
- Smoother zooming, panning, and gestures
- Modern dark UI and styling is much easier
- Better suited to match Unreal/Blender look and feel
- Since your current project already uses QML, this integrates more naturally
- Good performance with proper implementation

**Alternative**: Pure `QGraphicsView` (Widgets) – possible but less ideal for modern UI/feel.

---

## 2. Project Structure (Designed for Reusability)

```bash
MyNodeEditor/                      # ← Will become a reusable library
├── CMakeLists.txt
├── NodeEditor/
│   ├── include/NodeEditor/        # Public headers (clean API)
│   ├── src/                       # Private source
│   │   ├── backend/
│   │   ├── nodes/
│   │   └── ui/
│   ├── qml/                       # All QML components
│   └── resources.qrc
├── examples/
│   └── Demo/                      # Standalone demo application
└── MyMainProject/                 # ← Your existing or future project
```

---

## 3. Core Architecture

### Backend (C++)
- `GraphModel` – Pure data model (nodes + connections)
- `DataFlowEngine` – Topological sort + automatic data propagation
- `BaseNode` – Abstract base class with `compute()` virtual method
- Custom nodes inherit from `BaseNode`

### Frontend (QML)
- `NodeCanvas.qml` – Main interactive canvas
- `Node.qml` – Draggable node with header, body and ports
- `Edge.qml` – Smooth Bézier connections
- `Port.qml` – Input/output connection points (colored by type)
- `NodePalette.qml` + `PropertiesPanel.qml`

---

## 4. CMakeLists.txt (Library + Demo)

```cmake
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.22)
project(MyNodeEditor LANGUAGES CXX)

set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 20)
set(CMAKE_AUTOMOC ON)
set(CMAKE_AUTORCC ON)

find_package(Qt6 REQUIRED COMPONENTS
    Core Gui Qml Quick QuickControls2
)

qt_standard_project_setup()

# ====================== NODE EDITOR LIBRARY ======================
qt_add_library(NodeEditor STATIC)

qt_add_qml_module(NodeEditor
    URI "NodeEditor"
    VERSION 1.0
    QML_FILES
        qml/NodeCanvas.qml
        qml/Node.qml
        qml/Edge.qml
        qml/Port.qml
        qml/NodePalette.qml
        qml/PropertiesPanel.qml
    SOURCES
        src/backend/GraphModel.cpp
        src/backend/DataFlowEngine.cpp
        src/nodes/BaseNode.cpp
        # Add your custom nodes here
)

target_include_directories(NodeEditor PUBLIC
    ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/NodeEditor/include
)

target_link_libraries(NodeEditor PRIVATE
    Qt6::Core Qt6::Gui Qt6::Qml Qt6::Quick
)

# ====================== DEMO APPLICATION ======================
qt_add_executable(NodeEditorDemo
    examples/Demo/main.cpp
)

target_link_libraries(NodeEditorDemo PRIVATE NodeEditor)
```

---

## 5. Step-by-Step Implementation Plan

### Phase 1 – Foundation
1. Create project with the structure and CMake above
2. Implement basic `ApplicationWindow` with `SplitView` (Palette | Canvas | Properties)
3. Create draggable `Node.qml` component
4. Implement `Port.qml` and basic connection logic

### Phase 2 – Core Features
5. Build smooth zoomable canvas with grid background
6. Implement Bézier `Edge.qml`
7. Create `GraphModel` and expose it to QML
8. Build `DataFlowEngine` with automatic propagation

### Phase 3 – Polish & Integration
9. Node palette with drag & drop creation
10. Properties panel (dynamic)
11. Dark professional styling
12. JSON save/load + undo/redo (optional)

---

## 6. Data Flow & Propagation

- Nodes use `QVariant` (or custom typed variant) for inputs/outputs
- `DataFlowEngine` detects changes → marks downstream nodes dirty
- Executes nodes in topological order (Kahn’s algorithm recommended)
- Signals update QML visuals automatically

---

## 7. Styling (Unreal/Blender Style)

- Background: `#1E1E1E`
- Node header: Type-specific accent color
- Node body: `#2A2A2A`
- Grid: subtle lines `#333333`
- Selection: `#00B4FF`
- Connection curves: thickness + glow on hover

---

## 8. Future Integration into Your Main Project

**Yes – this design is made for easy integration.**

**How to embed later**:

```qml
import NodeEditor 1.0

NodeCanvas {
    width: parent.width
    height: parent.height
    graphModel: myExternalGraphModel   // You can pass your own model
}
```

You will be able to:
- Import the `NodeEditor` module into any QML project
- Register custom nodes from your main application
- Embed the canvas as a component
- Communicate bidirectionally with the graph

---

## 9. Build Instructions

```bash
mkdir build && cd build

# Linux/macOS
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
cmake --build . --parallel

# Windows
cmake .. -G "Visual Studio 17 2022" -A x64
cmake --build . --config Debug
```

Run the demo: `./NodeEditorDemo`

